the first minister of scotland nicolas  sturgeon has urged the scottish football  club wraith rovers to think again about  its decision to sign a player who is  civil court ruled to be a rapist  i'm joined now by judy hamilton who's  the chair of the rape rovers community  foundation and the labour councillor for  caldi central judy thank you so much for  talking to us when was the first you  heard that um david had been signed to  the club  yes i first heard the news very late on  monday evening very at the last minute  of the transfer window i read it on  social media and what was your reaction  and indeed the community's reaction  i was very shocked to see it because it  had been flagged um  a few weeks ago it was flagged that raid  rovers might be looking at this  particular player and  there was already a reaction from that  and so i was very shocked to see that  they had gone through  with the signing and the reaction of the  community has come in in the last  day or two  they're absolutely  shocked and stunned  and  believe that it gives a a very very poor  message to all of our women and girls  are absolutely condemn the decision  to  um to sign this particular player at  this particular time  what do you make of the manager john  mcglynn saying that um david goodwill  had a proven track record as a goal  goalscorer and that  with signing him it was crucial to the  club's promotion push in our quest to  reach the sbfl premiership it is a great  signing for wraith rovers as a club as  david is the top goalscorer in scotland  uh i think that's very significant and  god only knows that we do need a striker  um however um this particular striker of  all the strikers in all the world was a  wrong decision and he comes with  quite a lot of baggage and this signing  comes with  a number of other things attached to it  and i don't believe that that had been  risk assessed or um  really that the community feeling had  not been really taken on board  which is disappointing because  raythrovers does  um  perform itself as a family club  and we are very  fond of our families of our women and  girls we're very proud of our women and  girls and we're very proud of all the  work that we've done so far and feel  that it's been undone in the stroke of a  pen  well there are many who would argue it's  not just the message it sends to the  women and girls but to everyone  yes it is a message to everyone and in  the community foundation we work with  families we work with older people  we work with um teenagers and we've  had this throughout our whole community  foundation has been absolutely  devastated by this  do you think it's damaging for the club  in the long run  i think it will be i think that these  24 48 hours have been very very damaging  indeed and will continue to be so  but i think some of the damage that has  been done is possibly going to be very  hard to undo  the club have also said that um it  completely respects differing views  on the subject do you think that's what  this is differing views  i don't think it's differing views i  think we condemn  violence against women and sexual abuse  of all kinds i think that's  across the board everybody would condemn  that i don't think that um differing  views on that subject whether there are  different views on the individual  um  maybe there are um you know um  the football club seem to be looking at  the on the park activity and the fact  that he's a good footballer a good  player and that i have no doubt that he  is um but the  shadow of violence against women that  follows him is something that can't be  tolerated  they've also said that they've been  engaging with this on fans and  stakeholders have they been engaging  with you  no we've had absolutely no consultation  whatsoever we were not invited to any  discussions and we've not had any  consultation whatsoever and really it's  the community foundation that has taken  the brunt of this we are the people who  look after families look after children  women and children and look after the  women and girls team um and the women  and girls program starting from the go  girls which are very very young little  girls and right up until the women and  girls team so um  i  reject the fact that they've had  engagement because that hasn't happened  nicholas sturgeon has urged the club to  think again just very briefly what do  you think should happen now  i  i would echo this those sentiments and  ask the club to really think about what  this has done to our community and on  what it's doing um and what the messages  that they're sending out um so yes  we do need to think again i recognize  that the decision around contracts and  things is a matter for the club um and  it's not for anybody else to decide  because there will be contractual and  legal issues around that um but i would  urge them to think very seriously about  the ramifications of this  okay judy thank you so much for talking  to us  thank you
 

